1
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:05,997
At a gathering, one person
usually recites the du'a aloud,

2
00:00:05,997 --> 00:00:09,674
while everyone else reads
along, from a book or a phone.

3
00:00:09,674 --> 00:00:13,948
Many du'as are long, this one,
Dua Tawassul, is 115 lines,

4
00:00:13,948 --> 00:00:16,767
and it's easy to lose your place.

5
00:00:16,767 --> 00:00:20,932
This app listens to the reciter and
tries to keep your place for you.

6
00:00:20,932 --> 00:00:23,555
To do that, it has two questions to answer.

7
00:00:23,555 --> 00:00:30,724
Which du'a is this, out of 522 du'as and
ziyarat, the greetings recited at shrines?

8
00:00:30,724 --> 00:00:33,850
And where in it is he right
now, down to the word?

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,783
Two models listen at the same
time, one for each question.

10
00:00:38,783 --> 00:00:44,143
Whisper, a speech recognizer, turns the last
few seconds into text every second or two,

11
00:00:44,143 --> 00:00:47,030
and that text is looked for in every du'a.

12
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:50,613
A much smaller letter model hears
letters ten times a second,

13
00:00:50,613 --> 00:00:53,974
and follows him through
the du'a Whisper found.

14
00:00:54,967 --> 00:00:57,338
Let's take one recording through both.

15
00:00:57,338 --> 00:01:02,838
It's Hussein Ghareeb, about five
minutes into Dua Tawassul, at line 38.

16
00:01:02,838 --> 00:01:05,422
Neither model heard him in training.

17
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:11,156
Every second or so, the app takes the last
six seconds of sound, called a window,

18
00:01:11,156 --> 00:01:13,217
and draws it as a spectrogram.

19
00:01:13,217 --> 00:01:18,541
Time runs left to right, pitch from low to
high, and the brighter a spot, the louder.

20
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:21,040
Whisper turns that into text.

21
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:24,157
The app's version is small
enough to run on a phone.

22
00:01:24,157 --> 00:01:27,339
Out of the box, it barely
understands recitation,

23
00:01:27,339 --> 00:01:31,949
where one word can be drawn out for
seconds, so it was trained further,

24
00:01:31,949 --> 00:01:37,467
on over 100 hours of recited du'as, and
on synthetic voices of ordinary people.

25
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:38,849
Here's what it heard.

26
00:01:38,849 --> 00:01:40,533
Yā wajīh ‘inda-llāh.

27
00:01:40,533 --> 00:01:42,848
What he actually recited was this.

28
00:01:42,848 --> 00:01:44,135
Yā wajīhan.

29
00:01:44,135 --> 00:01:48,706
Close, but not exact, and only half the
line, because he draws every word out.

30
00:01:48,706 --> 00:01:52,485
So nothing after this point
takes the text as certain.

31
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:57,722
The app compares the heard
text with every du'a it knows.

32
00:01:57,722 --> 00:02:01,876
Vowel marks, spellings and spaces
are simplified on both sides.

33
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:07,371
Then it counts the fewest letters to change,
add, or remove, to turn one into the other.

34
00:02:07,371 --> 00:02:09,742
Here it's one, a missing alif.

35
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:16,642
It does this ending at every one of
140,000 words, in about a hundredth of a second.

36
00:02:16,642 --> 00:02:22,080
Here are the costs across Dua Tawassul:
the shorter the bar, the closer the fit.

37
00:02:23,067 --> 00:02:27,382
Every word of every text gets a
probability that he's there right now.

38
00:02:27,382 --> 00:02:30,795
Before anything is heard, it's
spread over all the texts,

39
00:02:30,795 --> 00:02:34,089
with a little more on the
ones people recite most.

40
00:02:34,089 --> 00:02:37,678
With each window, places that
fit well become more likely,

41
00:02:37,678 --> 00:02:40,300
and places that fit badly much less.

42
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,133
Add up the probabilities inside each du'a.

43
00:02:43,133 --> 00:02:47,433
If you'd walked in at exactly
this moment, after one window,

44
00:02:47,433 --> 00:02:50,313
Dua Tawassul would be ahead, at 29%.

45
00:02:50,313 --> 00:02:53,751
The app names a du'a only
at 70%, and until then,

46
00:02:53,751 --> 00:02:57,067
it offers its best guesses for you to tap.

47
00:02:57,067 --> 00:03:02,299
A second later, it's at
99.8%, and the app names it.

48
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:15,136
The same probabilities also say
roughly where he is in the du'a.

49
00:03:15,136 --> 00:03:18,020
That's where the second model takes over.

50
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,242
Whisper alone can't keep up with the word.

51
00:03:21,242 --> 00:03:25,273
Its text comes only every second
or two, with no timing inside it,

52
00:03:25,273 --> 00:03:28,600
and describes sound that's
already a second old.

53
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,518
So the letter model listens alongside.

54
00:03:31,518 --> 00:03:34,369
It's the listening half of the same Whisper,

55
00:03:34,369 --> 00:03:39,441
with one small layer added that maps
what it hears onto the alphabet.

56
00:03:39,441 --> 00:03:43,358
Ten times a second, it takes
the last two seconds of sound,

57
00:03:43,358 --> 00:03:48,138
and for every fiftieth of a second,
gives a probability for each letter,

58
00:03:48,138 --> 00:03:50,331
or for no letter at all.

59
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:53,543
Here it is as he carries on.

60
00:03:53,543 --> 00:03:55,080
Ishfa‘ lanā ‘inda.

61
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:59,834
Each row is one letter of these
words, and time runs left to right.

62
00:03:59,834 --> 00:04:06,153
The brighter a square, the surer the model is
that it heard that letter, at that moment.

63
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:10,401
Two seconds hold a word or two,
and many du'as share words,

64
00:04:10,401 --> 00:04:13,604
so the letter model doesn't
search for the du'a.

65
00:04:13,604 --> 00:04:17,342
Instead, a decoder follows him
through the one Whisper found.

66
00:04:17,342 --> 00:04:22,158
It keeps a probability on every letter
of the du'a, not just the line he's on,

67
00:04:22,158 --> 00:04:25,352
and updates it every fiftieth of a second.

68
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:30,502
Each update asks two things, much
as you'd keep your place yourself.

69
00:04:30,502 --> 00:04:33,342
Where could he be now, given where he was?

70
00:04:33,342 --> 00:04:35,536
Reading on costs nothing.

71
00:04:35,536 --> 00:04:40,466
Starting the line again costs a little,
going back a line or three costs more,

72
00:04:40,466 --> 00:04:45,237
skipping ahead more still, and
anywhere else in the du'a the most.

73
00:04:45,237 --> 00:04:49,314
And which of those places
explains the letters just heard?

74
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:53,870
Talk that isn't the du'a, and the
salawat people say between lines,

75
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:58,216
have places of their own, so they
don't drag the highlight along.

76
00:04:58,216 --> 00:05:03,086
A pause is simply no letters, so the
highlight waits on the last word said.

77
00:05:03,367 --> 00:05:06,417
Here's what that looks like
when a reader goes back.

78
00:05:06,417 --> 00:05:11,139
We cut this recording so that after
line 43, he returns to line 41,

79
00:05:11,139 --> 00:05:13,833
as people reading along often do.

80
00:05:13,833 --> 00:05:17,046
While he takes a breath,
nothing new is heard,

81
00:05:17,046 --> 00:05:20,929
so the probability stays
at the end of line 43.

82
00:05:20,929 --> 00:05:26,425
When he starts again, the first sounds fit
several places, and for half a second,

83
00:05:26,425 --> 00:05:29,942
the highlight goes to the start of line 43.

84
00:05:29,942 --> 00:05:35,998
A few letters later, line 41 explains them
best, and the highlight follows him there,

85
00:05:35,998 --> 00:05:38,533
about a second after he began.

86
00:05:38,533 --> 00:05:42,669
Whisper keeps listening as a check,
every two seconds on a phone.

87
00:05:42,669 --> 00:05:46,038
If he moves to a different
du'a, Whisper notices first,

88
00:05:46,038 --> 00:05:51,171
and the decoder follows once Whisper
has held the new one for two seconds.

89
00:06:17,867 --> 00:06:22,503
So, Whisper and the probabilities over
every du'a find which du'a it is,

90
00:06:22,503 --> 00:06:23,962
and roughly where.

91
00:06:23,962 --> 00:06:28,377
The letter model and the decoder
follow him through it, word by word.

92
00:06:28,377 --> 00:06:30,935
All of it runs inside the phone's browser,

93
00:06:30,935 --> 00:06:34,398
so the recitation never
has to leave the phone.

94
00:06:35,367 --> 00:06:40,554
It was tested on voices kept out of training,
reading the way people do: in flow,

95
00:06:40,554 --> 00:06:44,141
pausing, talking in between,
going back, skipping ahead,

96
00:06:44,141 --> 00:06:46,854
in quiet rooms and echoing halls.

97
00:06:47,233 --> 00:06:51,371
It names the du'a within
10 seconds 90% of the time.

98
00:06:51,371 --> 00:06:55,128
The highlight is on the
reader's line 87% of the time,

99
00:06:55,128 --> 00:06:58,281
and on the exact word two times in three.

100
00:06:58,281 --> 00:07:00,622
When a reader goes back or skips ahead,

101
00:07:00,622 --> 00:07:04,533
it follows within three seconds
nearly three times in four.

102
00:07:04,533 --> 00:07:06,339
It still makes mistakes.

103
00:07:06,339 --> 00:07:08,981
When two du'as share a
passage word for word,

104
00:07:08,981 --> 00:07:11,982
it can't tell them apart
until they part ways.

105
00:07:11,982 --> 00:07:16,570
It does worse in echoing halls, and
when a reader jumps around the du'a.

106
00:07:16,570 --> 00:07:20,605
And for a du'a it doesn't have,
it too often shows one it does.

107
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,533
That's the whole path, from the
microphone to the highlighted word.
